WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:11.070
<v Ira Glass>Julia's 13. Jane and Ella are 14. And when they came to our studio for an interview, they did this thing they do all the time when they hang out. They take pictures of themselves. And-- no surprise-- they post them to Instagram.
<v Ira Glass>Julia 13岁。Jane和Ella 14岁。当她们来到我们的工作室接受采访时，她们做了她们平时待在一起时常做的事情。她们给自己拍照。而且——不出所料——她们把照片发到了Instagram上。

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.210
<v Ira Glass>There you go.
<v Ira Glass>给你。

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.680
<v Jane>No, retake it. It's really bad.
<v Jane>不行，重拍。这张太丑了。

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.240
<v Ella>Jane, calm down.
<v Ella>Jane，冷静点。

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.170
<v Julia>It doesn't matter, OK? This is what happens every time.
<v Julia>没关系，好吗？每次都这样。

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.470
<v Ella>I'm just gonna post it and we'll see--
<v Ella>我这就发上去，然后我们看看——

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:19.170
<v Jane>We'll see what happens.
<v Jane>我们看看会发生什么。

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.660
<v Ella>--how quickly everything comes in.
<v Ella>——看看大家回复得有多快。

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.110
<v Ira Glass>So what's your prediction on what's going to happen?
<v Ira Glass>那你预测会发生什么？

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:28.230
<v Ella>Usually there's at least-- usually there's two likes in a minute. But I don't know, because people might not be up yet.
<v Ella>通常至少会有——通常一分钟内会有两个赞。但我不知道，因为大家可能还没起床。

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:29.080
<v Jane>People aren't-- there's--
<v Jane>人们没有——有——

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:43.700
<v Ira Glass>It was 11:00 in the morning on a day with no school, not prime time for posting photos at all. Nighttime is usually when you get the most likes and comments. But, you know, 11:00 AM. A minute passes. No response on the photo they took in the studio. And then?
<v Ira Glass>那是放假期间上午11点，根本不是发照片的黄金时间。晚上通常是点赞和评论最多的时候。但是，你知道，上午11点。一分钟过去了。她们在工作室拍的照片没有得到任何回应。然后呢？

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.820
<v Ella>Oh, wait, three likes.
<v Ella>噢，等等，三个赞。

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.160
<v Julia>Oh, three likes.
<v Julia>噢，三个赞。

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:46.910
<v Jane>From Ro--
<v Jane>来自Ro——

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:53.430
<v Ella>From three people. No one's commented yet. One of these is my best friend. OK, another person liked it. Two people.
<v Ella>来自三个人。还没人评论。其中一个是我的好朋友。好的，又有人点赞了。两个人。

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.510
<v Jane>Two people. OK, we're getting a lot of likes now.
<v Jane>两个人。好的，我们现在收到很多赞了。

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:55.100
<v Ella>Three. Another person.
<v Ella>三个。又一个人。

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.550
<v Jane>How many likes do we have now?
<v Jane>我们现在有多少个赞了？

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:57.560
<v Ella>Six, I think.
<v Ella>我想是六个。

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.950
<v Jane>OK, we have one, two, three, four, five, six likes in a minute. That's actually good, Ella.
<v Jane>好的，一分钟内我们有六个赞。这其实不错了，Ella。

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:17.300
<v Ira Glass>What they're waiting for is not just likes and comments but a specific kind of comment. This is probably not going to be news to any of you who have teenage girls in your lives, but I bet lots of you do not know about this. They want comments from other girls, and they say the wording is pretty much always the same.
<v Ira Glass>她们等待的不仅仅是赞和评论，而是一种特定的评论。对于你们当中生活中接触过少女的人来说，这可能不是什么新闻，但我敢说你们很多人并不知道这一点。她们想要其他女孩的评论，而且她们说措辞几乎总是完全一样的。

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.730
<v Jane>Gorgeous. Pretty. Stunning.
<v Jane>华丽。漂亮。迷人。

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:20.760
<v Ella>Stunning? Yeah.
<v Ella>迷人？是啊。

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.820
<v Jane>You kill it. You're so pretty. So beautiful.
<v Jane>你美极了。你太漂亮了。太美了。

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:37.170
<v Ira Glass>OK, just to be clear, they say this about everybody's selfies, whether or not the selfies are, in fact, stunning or beautiful. This is super-affirming language that is applied equally to every girl, universally. You've heard of bullying online? This is the opposite.
<v Ira Glass>好的，说清楚点，她们会对每个人的自拍都这么说，不管这些自拍实际上是不是真的华丽或美丽。这是一种超级肯定的语言，被普遍地应用在每个女孩身上。听说过网络霸凌吗？这恰恰相反。

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:42.850
<v Ella>A lot of people don't just say pretty. You either say like, Pretty, WTF? Gorgeous, WTF? Perfect.
<v Ella>很多人不仅仅说漂亮。你要么说，漂亮，搞什么鬼？华丽，搞什么鬼？完美。

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:43.970
<v Julia>Like all caps sometimes.
<v Julia>有时全是大写字母。

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:48.100
<v Ella>Um, some people say, like, model. You can add OMG after anything.
<v Ella>嗯，有些人会说，比如，模特。你可以在任何词后面加OMG。

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.100
<v Jane>Cutest, like--
<v Jane>最可爱，像——

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:49.510
<v Julia>Yeah.
<v Julia>是啊。

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:50.460
<v Ella>Something "-est."
<v Ella>以“est”结尾的词。

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.990
<v Ira Glass>The one word you don't say? "Sexy." They explained you could say "hot," but never "sexy."
<v Ira Glass>唯一不能说的词？“性感”。她们解释说你可以说“火辣”，但绝不能说“性感”。

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.330
<v Jane>They are-- there's a different--
<v Jane>她们是——有不同的——

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:58.180
<v Ella>They're the same thing, actually.
<v Ella>其实它们是一回事。

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.400
<v Jane>There's a different connotation to "sexy" than there is to "hot."
<v Jane>“性感”和“火辣”的内涵是不一样的。

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.380
<v Ella>'Cause, like, it has the word "sex" in it. Sex.
<v Ella>因为，它里面有“性”这个词。性。

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:03.650
<v Julia>Yeah, I think it's the word.
<v Julia>是啊，我想就是这个词。

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:04.240
<v Ella>Like, intercourse.
<v Ella>比如，性交。

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:04.640
<v Jane>Ella!
<v Jane>Ella！

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:05.440
<v Jane>[GIRLS GIGGLE]
<v Jane>[女孩们咯咯笑]

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:17.780
<v Ira Glass>This is not about sex. It's not about boys. It's about girls, and friendship. And it's very repetitive-- the same phrases, over and over. I asked them each to pick a selfie that they'd posted and read the comments.
<v Ira Glass>这与性无关。这与男生无关。这关乎女孩，关乎友谊。而且它非常重复——同样的短语，一遍又一遍。我让她们每人挑一张发过的自拍，然后读出评论。

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.920
<v Jane>So I have gorgeous, pretty.
<v Jane>我有华丽，漂亮。

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.050
<v Ella>You're so pretty, OMG.
<v Ella>你太漂亮了，OMG。

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:21.450
<v Jane>So pretty.
<v Jane>太漂亮了。

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:22.630
<v Ella>This is so pretty, OMG.
<v Ella>这太漂亮了，OMG。

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:23.980
<v Jane>Heart eyes. So pretty. Heart eyes. Gorgeous. Gorge.
<v Jane>爱心眼。太漂亮了。爱心眼。华丽。美翻了。

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:24.920
<v Ella>You're so pretty, bye.
<v Ella>你太漂亮了，拜。

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:25.810
<v Jane>Cutest.
<v Jane>最可爱。

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:30.690
<v Ira Glass>They say their parents don't understand this. Ella's mom has Instagram, so she sees the pictures and the comments.
<v Ira Glass>她们说她们的父母不理解这一点。Ella的妈妈有Instagram，所以她能看到照片和评论。

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:37.830
<v Ella>She's just like, why? Why? Why is everyone doing this? Like, why are 50 people feeling the need to tell you that you're pretty?
<v Ella>她总是说，为什么？为什么？为什么每个人都非要这么做？为什么50个人都觉得有必要告诉你你很漂亮？

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.310
<v Ira Glass>Julia's mom? Same thing.
<v Ira Glass>Julia的妈妈？也是一样。

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:50.170
<v Julia>She's like, why? Why are all these people saying, like, oh, you're so perfect? And my dad just thinks it's funny. He's like, why are these people doing this? Like, it doesn't-- it's kind of stupid. They think it's, like, stupid.
<v Julia>她就像，为什么？为什么所有人都在说，噢，你太完美了？我爸爸觉得这很好笑。他就像，为什么这些人要这么做？这没什么——这有点愚蠢。他们觉得这很愚蠢。

00:02:50.170 --> 00:03:01.580
<v Ira Glass>So what's it about? Well, the answer to that question is complicated, and it involves going deep into an intricate language that's going on in the comments. Welcome to WBEZ Chicago. It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>那这到底是为了什么？嗯，那个问题的答案很复杂，而且需要深入了解评论中存在的一套错综复杂的语言。欢迎来到WBEZ芝加哥。这里是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:29.580
<v Ira Glass>Today's program is about status updates of various kinds-- the literal kind, like on Instagram. But most of our show today is about the kind of slow changes in status that happen to adults, that happen usually when people get money or they get recognition, where what happens is, you know, quiet, and slow, and really, is sometimes kind of rude to talk about. Like, when do you say to anybody, "Hey, you're making a lot more money than you used to." Or, "Hey, you're making a lot less money." Or, "You're making a lot more money than me."
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目是关于各种状态更新的——字面意义上的那种，比如在Instagram上。但我们今天节目的大部分是关于成年人生活中缓慢的状态改变，通常发生在人们有了钱或获得认可的时候，发生的事情是安静的、缓慢的，而且说实话，谈论这些有时有点粗鲁。比如，你什么时候会对别人说，“嘿，你比以前赚得多多了。”或者，“嘿，你赚得少多了。”或者，“你赚得比我多多了。”

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:51.640
<v Ira Glass>And that's why I wanted to talk to Jane and Ella and Julia. Because for them, this stuff is not gradual or vague or shameful to talk about. It's happening out in the open, online. They're online all the time, constantly getting updates on the social standing of everybody they know, in detail and at incredible speed. And that's what these Instagram comments are all about, if you know how to read them.
<v Ira Glass>这就是为什么我想和Jane、Ella还有Julia聊聊。因为对她们来说，这些东西不是渐进的、模糊的，或者谈论起来令人羞耻的。它在网上公开发生着。她们一直在线，不断地获取她们认识的每个人的社交地位更新，非常详尽且速度惊人。如果你懂得如何解读，这就是这些Instagram评论的全部含义。

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:58.730
<v Ira Glass>So let's just start right there, with Act 1. Act 1. Finding the Self in Selfie.
<v Ira Glass>所以让我们从这里开始，第一幕。第一幕。在自拍中寻找自我。

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:35.170
<v Ira Glass>So these comments-- you know, you're so beautiful, you're gorgeous, you're cutest-- which look utterly interchangeable to Julia and Ella and Jane's parents, they are in fact a very specific language that tells the girls all kinds of things. And a lot of the meaning in the comments actually has nothing to do with the actual words, whether they say gorgeous, or pretty, or OMG, or whatever. It's about who is doing the commenting, and whether that person likes a photo or comments on the photo-- liking a photo means something totally different than commenting. You comment with somebody you're close to, or who you're trying to get close to. And with a close friend, you kind of have to comment.
<v Ira Glass>所以这些评论——你知道，你太美了，你华丽，你是最可爱的——在Julia、Ella和Jane的父母看来完全是可以互换的，实际上却是一种非常具体的语言，告诉女孩们各种各样的事情。评论中很多含义其实与实际词汇无关，不管它们说的是华丽、漂亮、OMG还是其他什么。这关乎是谁在评论，以及那个人是给照片点赞还是评论照片——点赞和评论意味着完全不同的事情。你和亲近的人，或者你试图亲近的人评论。而对于亲密的朋友，你几乎必须评论。

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:47.420
<v Ella>It's definitely a social obligation, because you want to let them know, and also let people who are seeing those, that I have a close relationship with this person, so close that I can comment on their pictures, like, this is so cute, or, you look so great here.
<v Ella>这绝对是一种社交义务，因为你想让她们知道，同时也让看到这些评论的人知道，我和这个人有亲密的关系，亲密到我可以在她们的照片下评论，比如，这太可爱了，或者，你在这里看起来棒极了。

00:04:47.420 --> 00:05:11.830
<v Ira Glass>Then, of course, when you do post a comment about a picture, there's the whole politics of whether your friend is going to comment back to your comment. And subtle differences in the wording when they comment back could mean something, even though, to an outsider, the words basically look the same. Julia says that she'll comment back, oh my god, I love you so much, to a close friend who compliments a picture of hers. But she says with somebody you don't know so well, it goes differently.
<v Ira Glass>然后，当然，当你确实发了一条关于照片的评论时，你的朋友会不会回评你的评论还有一套政治学。即便在外人看来，这些词看起来基本一样，但她们回评时措辞上的细微差别可能意味着什么。Julia说，她会给赞美她照片的亲密朋友回评“天哪，我太爱你了”。但她说如果是和不那么熟的人，情况就不同了。

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:27.540
<v Julia>If they say, like, oh my god, like, you're so pretty, you say, OMG, but that's you. You know, like-- like you're the pretty one? I feel like that's something that I say to someone who's like-- who I'm not super close with, but I feel like-- like I have to say something back.
<v Julia>如果她们说，比如，天哪，你太漂亮了，你会说，OMG，但那是你。你知道，比如——好像你是那个漂亮的人？我觉得那是会对那种——我不那么亲近，但觉得——觉得我必须回点什么的人说的话。

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:40.310
<v Jane>Especially because we, like, just started high school, so we're meeting a lot of new people. So you would comment on someone's photo who you're not really super close with or that you don't know really well. And it's sort of a statement, like, I want to be friends with you, or I want to get to know you, or like, I think you're cool.
<v Jane>特别是因为我们，比如，刚上高中，所以我们在认识很多新人。所以你会给不太亲近或者不太认识的人的照片评论。这算是一种声明，比如，我想和你做朋友，或者我想了解你，或者，我觉得你很酷。

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:51.290
<v Jane>If someone that you don't know very well commented on your photo, you-- it's sort of like an unspoken agreement that you have to comment back on their photo. Like when you're making new friends, if they comment on your photo, you comment on their photo.
<v Jane>如果不认识的人评论了你的照片，你——这算是一种不成文的约定，你必须回评她们的照片。就像在结交新朋友时，如果她们评论了你的照片，你也会评论她们的照片。

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:52.560
<v Ella>It's like a chain reaction.
<v Ella>这就像连锁反应。

00:05:52.560 --> 00:06:10.650
<v Ira Glass>Another thing you don't see if you don't know this language of comments and commenting back-- let's say that you comment on somebody's photo. When they comment back, do they comment to you individually? Or do they group you in with 20 or 30 other people and send just one big group comment back to all of you? That tells you where you stand with the person.
<v Ira Glass>如果你不懂这种评论和回评的语言，你还看不到另一件事——假设你评论了某人的照片。当她们回评时，她们是单独评论你吗？还是把你和另外20或30个人归在一起，发一条统一的大组评论给你们所有人？那告诉你你在那个人心中的地位。

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:29.990
<v Ira Glass>Then there are all those situations where somebody choosing not to comment is a really big deal. Like for example, if you post a picture of yourself with a friend and then the friend does not comment on that picture, that's just cold. That is leaving you dangling. Or if you post a selfie, close friends are expected to chime in with support. When they don't?
<v Ira Glass>还有各种选择不评论的情况，那可是件大事。比如，如果你发了一张你和朋友的合影，然后那个朋友没有评论那张照片，那真是太冷漠了。那是让你悬在那儿。或者如果你发了一张自拍，亲密朋友被期望给予支持。当她们没这么做时？

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:35.750
<v Ella>You definitely feel insecure. Because, like, you expect them to comment, and they don't, and you're like, why?
<v Ella>你绝对会感到不安。因为，比如，你期待她们评论，但她们没有，你会想，为什么？

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:38.220
<v Julia>This isn't even good enough for, like, my best friend.
<v Julia>这甚至不够好，对于我的好朋友来说。

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:38.480
<v Ella>Yeah.
<v Ella>是啊。

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:39.980
<v Julia>Then you're like, oh, wait.
<v Julia>然后你会想，噢，等等。

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:51.530
<v Jane>Like, what if they've seen it  and they're just not liking it on purpose, or like, what if everybody who's seeing it thinks that we're not actually friends because they're not commenting on it. They haven't commented yet. It's been X amount of time and they still haven't liked it.
<v Jane>比如，要是她们看到了，但故意不点赞怎么办，或者，要是所有看到照片的人都觉得我们其实不是朋友，因为她们没有评论。她们还没评论。已经过了X那么久，她们还没点赞。

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:58.330
<v Julia>It's this kind of shallow-- like, why are you not responding to something that I never said you had to respond to in the first place?
<v Julia>这是一种浅薄的——比如，你为什么要对一件我本来就没要求你回复的事情不回复呢？

00:06:58.330 --> 00:06:59.310
<v Julia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Julia>[笑声]

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:03.400
<v Ira Glass>And tell me about how quickly people have to respond. Like, what's the normal amount of time?
<v Ira Glass>告诉我人们必须回复得有多快。比如，正常的时长是多少？

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.070
<v Ella>Within 10 minutes, I think.
<v Ella>十分钟内，我想。

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:05.240
<v Jane>Yeah, usually.
<v Jane>是啊，通常。

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.560
<v Julia>People are always on Instagram. Everyone's always on Instagram.
<v Julia>人们总是在Instagram上。每个人总是在Instagram上。

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.320
<v Jane>There's definitely a weird psychology to it.
<v Jane>这绝对有一种奇怪的心理。

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:10.140
<v Julia>Yeah.
<v Julia>是啊。

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:10.990
<v Ella>Definitely.
<v Ella>绝对。

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:14.450
<v Jane>It's just sort of the way it is. It's like unspoken rules that everybody knows and follows.
<v Jane>这只是现状。这就像大家都知道并遵守的不成文规则。

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:23.370
<v Ira Glass>But can I ask you, have you gotten caught up in weird mind games where somebody commented or liked or didn't comment or didn't like, and then you're just like, I don't-- what does this-- what does this one means?
<v Ira Glass>但我能问问你，你有没有陷入过奇怪的心理博弈，就是有人评论或点赞，或者没评论或没点赞，然后你就觉得，我不知道——这到底意味着什么？

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:24.570
<v Ella>Yeah. Someone like--
<v Ella>是啊。有人比如——

